    │
 16 │ ╭─▶       (match c with
    ┆ ┆   
 18 │ ├─▶          | Succ b => reach flag k b d)
    │ │                                            
    │ ╰──────────────────────────────────────────── this `match`
    │ 
 26 │       match flag c with
    │             ───┬──  
    │                ╰──── this `match` replaced its scrutinee with `False` in this branch
    │ 
 28 │         | False => n => andRight b (reach flag (Succ k) c d) hc
    │                                                              ─┬  
    │                                                               ╰── expected `(b && (if flag c then (match c with …) || reach flag k (c + 1) d else reach flag k c d)) = True`, but this has type `(b && reach flag k c d) = True`
    │     
    │     Note 1: they first differ at `if flag c then (match c with …) || reach flag k (c + 1) d else reach flag k c d` against `reach flag k c d`
    │     
    │     Note 2: this was refined by the `match`: it has `False` where what's expected has the scrutinee. The goal and the hypotheses are refined; what's expected comes from terms written in the branch (a lemma's premise, or an explicit argument), which aren't. Write `_` for such an argument, so it is inferred from this
    │     
    │     Note 3: name the `match`'s equation, `match h: … with`: a proof whose type has the scrutinee where this branch has its value, or the other way round, is then rewritten along it where the rewritten type still checks (otherwise `rewrite h at …` says why not). Or bind the lemma with `let` before the `match`, so it is refined like the hypotheses
    │     
    │     Note 4: this expects the test fact `(b && (if flag c then (match c with …) || reach flag k (c + 1) d else reach flag k c d)) = True`, which reads as `b ∧ (if flag c then (match c with …) || reach flag k (c + 1) d else reach flag k c d)`: build it part by part with `Both`, each part from a proof of its own reading
    │     
    │     Note 5: `(match …)` is a `match` written inside `reach`, marked "this `match`" above; the compiler turned it into a function that source can't name. The same `match` written again is another such function, equal to it only if neither calls the definition it's written in. To state a lemma about it, make it a local `def` in its arm and refer to it as `owner.name`, where `owner` is the definition it is written in (docs/syntax.md, "Proving things about a definition with an inner `match`")
